      "latency_ms": 2582.4,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 754,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, to understand water is to understand the lifeblood of our planet. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Three States of Matter\nWater is famous for being the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard state of water, found in oceans, rivers, rain, and our taps.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). \n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water evaporates or boils at 100°C (212°F).\n\n### 2. Unique Chemical Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unusual scientific properties that make it essential for life:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it acts as a carrier, transporting vital chemicals, minerals, and nutrients wherever it goes—whether through our bloodstream or through the soil to plants.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense as ice. This is why ice floats on top of lakes and oceans, insulating the water below and allowing marine life to survive the winter.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps human bodies from overheating.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which is how water moves up the roots of giant trees against gravity).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **Earth is a Water Planet:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered in water, mostly by oceans.\n*   **The Scarcity:** Despite the vast amount of water on Earth, **96.5% of it is saltwater** in the oceans. Only about **2.5% is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use.\n\n### 4. Why Water Matters for Life\nWater is the defining requirement for life as we know it. \n*   **Biology:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. Our brains are about 73% water, our lungs about 83%, and even our bones are roughly 31% water. We can survive weeks without food, but only a few days without water.\n*   **Ecosystems:** Every living thing—from the smallest bacterium to the largest blue whale—relies on water to survive, grow, and reproduce.\n*   **Civilization:** Human history has been shaped by water. Every major ancient civilization (like Mesopotamia, Ancient Egypt, and the Indus Valley) rose along the banks of major rivers. Today, water is essential for agriculture, industry, energy production, and sanitation.\n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with miraculous properties that makes the existence of our living, breathing world possible."
